In .NET MAUI, you use the **lifecycle mapping** API in `CreateMauiApp()` to hook platform events:

```csharp
public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
{
    var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
    builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

    builder.ConfigureLifecycleEvents(events =>
    {
#if ANDROID
        events.AddAndroid(android => android
            .OnPause(activity =>
            {
                // Runs when the activity is pausing
            })
            .OnStop(activity =>
            {
                // Runs when the activity is no longer visible
            }));
#endif

#if IOS
        events.AddiOS(ios => ios
            .DidEnterBackground(app =>
            {
                // Runs when the app enters background
            }));
#endif
    });

    return builder.Build();
}
```

**Key points:**

- Add `using Microsoft.Maui.LifecycleEvents;`
- Use `#if ANDROID` / `#if IOS` preprocessor directives to keep it platform-safe.
- `OnPause` ≈ partially obscured; `OnStop` ≈ fully hidden. On iOS, `DidEnterBackground` covers both.
- Other available hooks include `OnResume`, `OnDestroy` (Android) and `WillEnterForeground`, `WillTerminate` (iOS).
